           "source": "thm-implicit-midpoint-branch-symplectic-order-and-error",
           "source_section": "Statement",
-          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2d}$ be open, $H\\in C^3(U)$, and $F=J\\nabla H$. Suppose, uniformly for initial values $z$ in a compact convex tube, that $\\overline B(z,\\rho)\\subset U$, $\\|F\\|\\le M$, $\\|DF\\|\\le L_0$ on these balls, and $|h|M/2\\le\\rho$, $|h|L_0/2<1$. The exact implicit midpoint branch [[alg-implicit-midpoint-hamiltonian-step]] then exists uniquely in each ball. Its local map $\\Psi_h(z)=2Z-z$ is $C^1$ and satisfies $D\\Psi_h(z)^TJ D\\Psi_h(z)=J$.\n\nAssume also that the exact and numerical trajectories, the branch balls, and the segments used in comparison lie in a compact convex subset of $U$ for $nh\\le T$, with the displayed derivatives uniformly bounded. Then for some constants $C,L_1$ independent of $h,n$,\n$$\\|\\Psi_h(z)-\\varphi_h(z)\\|\\le Ch^3,\\qquad e_n\\le e^{L_1T}e_0+CTe^{L_1T}h^2,$$\nwhere $\\varphi_h$ is the exact Hamiltonian flow and $e_n=\\|z_n-\\varphi_{nh}(z_0)\\|$.",
+          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2d}$ be open, $H\\in C^2(U)$, $z=(q,p)$, and $J=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I\\\\-I&0\\end{pmatrix}$; define $F(q,p)=(\\nabla_pH(q,p),-\\nabla_qH(q,p))=J\\nabla H(q,p)$. For a signed step $h$, suppose a declared closed ball $\\overline B(z,\\rho)\\subset U$ has bounds $\\|F\\|\\le M$ and $\\|DF\\|\\le L$ satisfying $|h|M/2\\le\\rho$ and $\\theta:=|h|L/2<1$. The exact implicit midpoint branch [[alg-implicit-midpoint-hamiltonian-step]] then has a unique stage root $Z$ in this ball. The exact iterates $Z_0=z$, $Z_{k+1}=z+(h/2)F(Z_k)$ stay in the ball, converge to $Z$, and for every $k\\in\\mathbb N_0$ satisfy\n$$\\|Z_k-Z\\|\\le b_k:=\\frac{\\theta^k\\|Z_1-Z_0\\|}{1-\\theta},\\qquad \\|(2Z_k-z)-(2Z-z)\\|\\le2b_k.$$\nHere $\\theta^0=1$, including when $\\theta=0$; if $\\theta=0$, the first update equals $Z$ and $b_k=0$ for $k\\ge1$. Also $\\|(2Z-z)-z\\|\\le2\\rho$, which supplies an output-error bound when no update is computed.\n\nAssume additionally $H\\in C^3(U)$ and that these ball hypotheses hold uniformly for initial values $z$ in a compact convex tube, with common $\\rho,M,L$. At every admitted initial value the selected branch is locally the restriction of a $C^1$ solution branch, and its exact local map $\\Psi_h(z)=2Z-z$ satisfies $D\\Psi_h(z)^TJ D\\Psi_h(z)=J$.\n\nUnder these additional $C^3$ and uniform branch hypotheses, assume also that the exact and numerical trajectories, the branch balls, and the segments used in comparison lie in a compact convex subset of $U$ whenever $n|h|\\le T$, with the displayed derivatives uniformly bounded. Then for some constants $C,L_1$ independent of $h,n$,\n$$\\|\\Psi_h(z)-\\varphi_h(z)\\|\\le C|h|^3,\\qquad e_n\\le e^{L_1T}e_0+CTe^{L_1T}|h|^2,$$\nwhere $\\varphi_h$ is the exact Hamiltonian flow and $e_n=\\|z_n-\\varphi_{nh}(z_0)\\|$. These symplecticity and trajectory-error conclusions concern the exact selected map; no such conclusion is asserted here for a finite iteration output.",
